<div class="mw-parser-output"><h1>Consider</h1>
<p>Skill
Class: Assassin (guild level 0), Barbarian (guild level 0), Conjurer (guild level 0, up to 80%), Dark Knight (guild level 0, up to 90%), Druid (guild level 0, up to 85%), Fighter (guild level 0), Healer (guild level 3, up to 80%), Paladin (guild level 0, up to 90%), Ranger (guild level 0, up to 90%), Sorcerer (guild level 3, up to 60%), Thief (guild level 0, up to 80%)
Also taught by: Gustav, in the back room of the inn in Udgaard (anyone, up to 35%); Brother Johannes, at the monastery in the vineyards near Nymgaard (from level 40)
</p><p>Usage:
</p>
<pre>consider &lt;character&gt;
</pre>
<p>Example:
</p>
<pre>consider beast
</pre>
<p>If you are wondering whether an opponent is too strong for you, use this command
to see if you would have even the slightest chance. Some monsters are simply so
strong that they may kill you in one hit, and this command will help you know.
It judges how many rounds the opponent's blows would need to kill you, from
"RUN AWAY!" to "Plenty of time to flee.", and warns you when it is ten or more
levels above you.
</p><p>Be aware that it doesn't consider whether nearby monsters will help the one you
attack, or whether it has special attacks like a breath weapon or spells: these
are the risks you must run. It assumes you have full hit points, and it doesn't
count others who may help you. You need to have practised consider to use it,
but how well you know it doesn't change the answer.
</p><p><br />
See Also:
</p>
<pre>&gt; <a cmd='#'>Help Kill</a>
&gt; <a cmd='#'>Help Fleeing</a>
&gt; <a cmd='#'>Help Diagnostics</a>
</pre>




</div>